{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "add_term",
    "parent_shortcut": "fixed_environment",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "interaction_emergence",
      "parent_relation": "v_orbit depends only on M and r through an inverse-radius law with a small fixed oblateness correction proportional to R_body^2/r^2.",
      "child_relation": "v_orbit depends on both gravity and an independently varied environmental rotation rate, producing a mixed gravity-rotation balance so the required circular speed changes with the sign and magnitude of central spin relative to orbital radius.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "M",
        "r"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "At fixed M and r, increasing central rotation lowers the needed prograde equatorial orbital speed; the parent predicts no change because it has no rotation dependence."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "grav_scale",
        "expression": "G*M/r",
        "role": "baseline Keplerian speed-squared scale"
      },
      {
        "name": "shape_ratio",
        "expression": "R_body**2/r**2",
        "role": "strength of finite-size/oblateness contribution at the orbit"
      },
      {
        "name": "compactness_like",
        "expression": "G*M/(r)",
        "role": "central attraction level controlling whether rotation-induced corrections are detectable near the body"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Equatorial circular orbits close enough to the central body that rotation-induced frame dragging is not negligible compared with the Newtonian term, while still outside the body: r > R_body and the rotation correction remains subdominant so bounded circular motion persists.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "Within training, varying r near the body reveals a radius-dependent departure from the parent beyond the fixed oblateness trend; in holdout, extending to stronger central rotation preserves the same monotone gravity-rotation interaction rather than a pure rescaling.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent law in the non-rotating limit of the central body, where the spin-induced correction vanishes.",
    "freedom_change": "add_input",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "The missing mechanism is central-body rotation. A fixed parameter cannot reveal its response relationship because the benchmark must vary the source spin across samples to make the target mapping observably depend on it.",
    "gap_statement": "The current model includes static shape-induced modification of gravity but omits the effect of central-body rotation on the orbital speed of nearby circular equatorial motion.",
    "baseline_assumption": "The environment is effectively non-rotating, so only mass and static oblateness determine the circular-speed requirement.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Rotation of the massive compact body drags nearby spacetime or, in a weak-field effective description, introduces a spin-dependent correction to the circular-orbit speed distinct from the static oblateness term.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "M",
      "r",
      "R_body",
      "oblateness_coeff"
    ],
    "rationale": "Parent response relationship: with G, R_body, and oblateness_coeff fixed, varying r at fixed M gives one monotone speed curve set entirely by gravity plus a small static shape correction; varying any unmodeled central spin does nothing because the parent has no such dependence. Child change: release central rotation as an independent condition and add its coupling to the orbital balance, so at fixed M and r the target mapping changes with source spin, and across r this spin effect strengthens nearer the body. Concrete distinguishing condition: compare two datasets with the same M, r, R_body and shared refitted parent parameters but different central rotation rates in admissible near-body equatorial orbits. The child predicts different v_orbit values, with the contrast increasing as r decreases; the parent with one shared parameter set predicts identical values because spin is absent. This is a changed target mapping, not merely an auxiliary trajectory change, since the scored output itself varies with the new condition. Activation is scientifically admissible for compact rotating bodies and orbits outside the surface where the correction is noticeable yet still smaller than the leading attraction. Limitation: if all sampled systems are effectively non-rotating or very far from the body, the refinement would be weakly observable."
  },
